        -:  810: *	processor's cache line size. Any slot is only ever used by one thread.
        -:  811: *	This alignment guarantees that there will be no contention or cache
        -:  812: *	thrashing as threads update their own slot info, and also eliminates
        -:  813: *	any need for locking when accessing a slot.
        -:  814: *
        -:  815: *	A writer thread will scan every slot in the table to determine the oldest
        -:  816: *	outstanding reader transaction. Any freed pages older than this will be
        -:  817: *	reclaimed by the writer. The writer doesn't use any locks when scanning
        -:  818: *	this table. This means that there's no guarantee that the writer will
        -:  819: *	see the most up-to-date reader info, but that's not required for correct
        -:  820: *	operation - all we need is to know the upper bound on the oldest reader,
        -:  821: *	we don't care at all about the newest reader. So the only consequence of
